<!--
  /reference — the actual reference.

  What was here: thirteen sections, each one paragraph and ONE code block holding
  six unrelated constructs, every one of them "named" by a comment INSIDE the
  code. Nothing was scannable, nothing had a signature, nothing had an anchor —
  and it still taught controller hooks, ref(), scoped styles, and a paired
  .controller.ts, none of which exist.

  A reference is a lookup surface. One construct, one heading, one signature, one
  example. You should be able to Ctrl-F `bind:checked` and land on it.
-->
<column class="doc" gap="4">
  <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
  <h1>Reference</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every directive, block, and primitive the language has — one entry each.
    If it is here, it compiles: every sample on this page is built by the
    compiler on every commit.
  </p>

  <!-- ── TEMPLATE DIRECTIVES ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Template directives</h2>
  <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
    Eleven, and that is all of them. There is no twelfth way to do any of this.
  </p>

  <h3><code>&#123;expr&#125;</code> — text</h3>
  <p>Read a signal, or any expression, straight into the markup.</p>
  <code-block code={code.text}></code-block>

  <h3><code>attr=&#123;expr&#125;</code> — attribute</h3>
  <p>A dynamic attribute. Native elements take attributes; custom elements route to a property.</p>
  <code-block code={code.attr}></code-block>

  <h3><code>on:event</code> — events</h3>
  <p>
    Any DOM event. Modifiers declare intent instead of boilerplate:
    <code>|prevent</code>, <code>|stop</code>, <code>|self</code>,
    <code>|trusted</code>, <code>|key:Escape</code>, <code>|debounce:200</code>.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.on}></code-block>

  <h3><code>bind:value</code> — two-way, text</h3>
  <p>An input and a signal, kept in sync. Sugar for a read plus an <code>on:input</code> write.</p>
  <code-block code={code.bindValue}></code-block>

  <h3><code>bind:checked</code> — two-way, boolean</h3>
  <p>The same, for a checkbox or radio.</p>
  <code-block code={code.bindChecked}></code-block>

  <h3><code>bind:this</code> — the element</h3>
  <p>
    Hands you the node itself. Reach for <a href="/behavior"><code>use:</code></a>
    first — an action <em>is</em> the handle, and it cleans up after itself. This
    is for when you genuinely need the element as a <em>value</em>.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.bindThis}></code-block>

  <h3><code>class:name</code> — conditional class</h3>
  <p>Toggle a class when the expression is truthy.</p>
  <code-block code={code.classDir}></code-block>

  <h3><code>style:--prop</code> — a custom property</h3>
  <p>Set a CSS custom property. Only custom properties — this is not a way back to CSS.</p>
  <code-block code={code.styleDir}></code-block>

  <h3><code>prop:name</code> — pass a property</h3>
  <p>Force a value onto a child element as a property rather than an attribute.</p>
  <code-block code={code.propDir}></code-block>

  <h3><code>use:action</code> — behavior</h3>
  <p>
    Attach a function to an element. This is how every L1 element gets its
    behavior. <a href="/behavior">Behavior →</a>
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.useDir}></code-block>

  <h3><code>motion:trigger</code> — animation</h3>
  <p>
    Eight triggers, nine presets, one system. <a href="/motion">Motion →</a>
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.motionDir}></code-block>

  <!-- ── CONTROL FLOW ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Control flow</h2>
  <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
    Six blocks. Each is a partial — it splices into the surrounding markup with
    no wrapper element.
  </p>

  <h3><code>&#123;#if&#125;</code></h3>
  <code-block code={code.ifBlock}></code-block>

  <h3><code>&#123;#for&#125;</code></h3>
  <p><code>key</code> is required. Without it a reorder is a re-render.</p>
  <code-block code={code.forBlock}></code-block>

  <h3><code>&#123;#match&#125;</code></h3>
  <p>A discriminated match — exhaustive, and it reads like the data.</p>
  <code-block code={code.matchBlock}></code-block>

  <h3><code>&#123;#async&#125;</code></h3>
  <p>A live handle<p>A promise's three states, in the template.</p>apos;s three states in one block — loading, error, ok. It desugars into the two below.</p>
  <code-block code={code.asyncBlock}></code-block>

  <h3><code>&#123;#while-loading&#125;</code> · <code>&#123;#while-error&#125;</code></h3>
  <p>
    The loading and error branches of a live handle. The loaded value is only
    reachable through <code>kind === 'ok'</code>, so a template cannot render
    data it has not confirmed arrived.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.whileBlocks}></code-block>

  <!-- ── DECLARATIONS ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Declarations</h2>
  <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
    Two blocks at the top of a <code>.sui</code>. There is no third.
  </p>

  <h3><code>&lt;component&gt;</code> — what comes in</h3>
  <p>
    Props, and their types. A <strong>variant is a prop</strong> with a union
    type — there is no separate variants block, and the CSS keys off the
    attribute.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.componentBlock}></code-block>

  <h3><code>&lt;controller&gt;</code> — state and logic</h3>
  <p>
    One file. <code>scope()</code> returns the state the template reads;
    <code>mount()</code> runs once, after the element is in the DOM.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.controllerBlock}></code-block>

  <h3><code>&lt;slot&gt;</code> — the composition seam</h3>
  <code-block code={code.slots}></code-block>

  <alert tone="info">
    <strong>There is no <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>.</strong> Authored CSS is a
    compile error in every SUI file. <a href="/styling">Styling →</a>
  </alert>

  <!-- ── RUNTIME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Runtime</h2>
  <p sx="fg:muted-foreground">
    Imported from <code>@ship/sui-runtime</code>. Small on purpose.
  </p>

  <h3>Signals</h3>
  <p>The whole reactive core: a value, a computed value, and a side effect.</p>
  <code-block code={code.signals}></code-block>

  <h3>Live data</h3>
  <p>A server value as a three-state handle — loading, error, ok.</p>
  <code-block code={code.liveData}></code-block>

  <h3>Compositions</h3>
  <p>
    A plain function over the primitives. No type, no registration, no rules — if
    it touches the DOM it is an <a href="/behavior">action</a>; if it returns
    state it is a composition. <a href="/composition">Compositions →</a>
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.compositions}></code-block>

  <h3>Context</h3>
  <p>Push a signal down a subtree without threading it through every prop.</p>
  <code-block code={code.context}></code-block>

  <h3>Actions</h3>
  <p>The type behind <code>use:</code>. A function over an element, returning its undo.</p>
  <code-block code={code.action}></code-block>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/tooling">← Tooling</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/why">Why SUI →</a>
  </row>
</column>
